  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led until it was too late to matter.",
    "what_this_means": "Lawrence Butler tied it in the eighth with a single. The bullpen held. Blewett threw two scoreless, Medina and Harris each got through clean. And the game stayed locked. Then Baltimore scored twice in the tenth. That was the game. They finished 3-4 at home, never ahead after the second inning, converting one of six RISP situations into a run. Jacob Lopez gave them five innings, three hits, one run. Clean work. The relief corps did not give them anything else. But the offense could not find a moment when it mattered most. They had chances early and let them die. They got close late and ran out of outs.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like when it costs you. They didn't fail catastrophically. They failed methodically, one missed inning at a time. A two-run deficit after the second. Empty at-bats in the sixth when runners were in scoring position. The bats touched the ball enough to stay alive but not enough to take over. Then Lawrence Butler found a moment, tied it, and they had their window. But the tenth inning closed it. A one-run game is not a plan. They've now lost one straight after two wins. Fifteen games back. The division gap grew because they couldn't finish what their pitching started.",
